+
+# JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386) applyMergePatch
+
+## Request
+
+Some of our clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format. Implement JSON Merge
+Patch, as specified in RFC 7386.
+
+Export a function `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a merge patch to a
+target JSON document and returns the merged document.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- The merge follows RFC 7386, section 2: when the patch is an object, each member is merged into the
+  target; a member whose value is `null` removes the member from the target, and any other member is
+  merged recursively. When the patch is not an object (including an array or `null`), it replaces the
+  target entirely.
+- A target that is not an object is treated as `{}` before a patch object is merged into it.
+- Arrays are replaced as a whole, never merged element by element; `null` elements inside an array are
+  kept.
+- Removing a member that does not exist is not an error.
+- A member named `__proto__` is an ordinary member, and does not modify `Object.prototype`.
+- The patch must be a JSON value; `undefined`, `NaN`, an infinity and other non-JSON values throw.
+- The input target and patch are not mutated; the result shares no mutable state with them.
+- Tests cover each example of RFC 7386, appendix A, and the edge and error cases above, with `npm test`
+  (`node --test`).
+- The README documents the function, with an example, as it does for the other functions.
+
+## Requirement
+
+Standard: RFC 7386, JSON Merge Patch. The library must follow the published standard it implements.
